  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure is expected to shift east of the area today,\nmaintaining dry weather. A shortwave trough and surface cold \nfront are progged to advance across the Upper Great Lakes to the\nMidwest region later today into tonight. Ongoing showers and \nthunderstorms across western Ohio late this afternoon and \nevening are expected to weaken as they outrun more favorable \ninstability, though a few showers or a thunderstorm could reach \nas far east as eastern Ohio/western Pennsylvania overnight as a \nlead shortwave trough approaches.\n\nThe initial shortwave is progged to cross central and eastern PA\nThursday afternoon and evening, where more favorable shear and\ninstability for a severe weather potential is expected. The\nsurface cold front is expected to approach and cross the Upper\nOhio Valley region Thursday afternoon and evening, with\nscattered showers and thunderstorms expected. ML CAPE is\nexpected to range from 1000-1500 j/kg by afternoon, with around\n20kt of 0-6km shear. Some model soundings indicate mid level dry\nair as well, which could increase DCAPE values and create a\nmarginal damaging wind potential, mainly across wrn PA and nrn\nWV. SPC has maintained a Marginal Risk for severe storms across\nthis area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe cold front is expected to exit the region Thursday\nevening/night, with showers and thunderstorms ending. Dry\nweather should then return Friday through the weekend as surface\nhigh pressure builds under broad upper troughing aloft. The \nnext upper trough is progged to approach the region Tuesday,\nthough ensembles differ on the strength of the trough. Shower\nand thunderstorm chances should increase with the approaching\ntrough.",
